/**
@page getting_started Getting Started

@tableofcontents

This page is a hands-on introduction to Mercury. It takes you from a fresh
checkout to a small project that runs in the editor and in the player. If you
only want the reference for a class, start at the
<a href="namespaces.html">Namespaces</a> or
<a href="annotated.html">Classes</a> pages; every public
type has a page generated from its header.

@section getting_started_what What Mercury is

Mercury is a C++ engine for simulation games (think SimCity or Railroad
Tycoon), not a general-purpose 3D engine. It provides:

- a Vulkan 1.3 renderer with lighting, shadows, global illumination,
  post-processing and a UI renderer;
- a fixed-step game loop, input, physics (Jolt), audio (miniaudio), animation
  and asset loading;
- **the Mercury Editor**, a wxWidgets application for making projects; and
- **AngelScript and C++** as gameplay languages. AngelScript scripts are
  compiled by an embedded engine; C++ components are linked into a per-project
  executable. Both use the same component model, reflection and map format.

For a beginner, the quickest path is AngelScript: a project (`.mproj`), a map
(`.mmap`) and `.as` files, run by the shared `mercury_player`. C++ scripts use
the same `Behaviour` callbacks but need a small CMake target; see
@ref getting_started_cpp "Writing gameplay in C++".

@section getting_started_requirements Requirements

| Platform | Renderer | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Windows 10/11 | Vulkan | Renderer supported; the setup and build scripts currently target macOS and Linux |
| Linux (X11/Wayland) | Vulkan | Supported |
| macOS (Apple Silicon) | MoltenVK | Supported |

You need a GPU and driver with Vulkan 1.3 and dynamic rendering, plus
[CMake](https://cmake.org), [Ninja](https://ninja-build.org), a C++20 compiler
and the Rust toolchain. On macOS the minimum is macOS 26. Follow the more detailed platform steps in the project
<a href="index.html">README</a> or run the setup script, which installs the
toolchain, Vulkan loader, MoltenVK, DXC and the other development packages:

@code{.sh}
./scripts/setup_project.pl
@endcode

@section getting_started_build Build and run an example

Build the player and run the Pong example:

@code{.sh}
cmake --preset release
cmake --build --preset release
cmake --build --preset check            # build and run the tests
./build/release/bin/mercury_player examples/pong/pong.mproj
@endcode

Close the game window (or press Escape, where the example supports it) to
stop it.

Other complete examples live in `examples/`:

- `pong` -- 2D, the smallest complete game;
- `roll_a_ball` -- 3D physics, input actions and a HUD;
- `breakout` and `space_invaders` -- 2D arcade games;
- `monkey_ball` -- 3D stage with a pause menu.

@section getting_started_editor Open the editor

The editor is a separate executable. Give it a project, or no argument to see
the project launcher:

@code{.sh}
./build/release/bin/mercury_editor examples/pong/pong.mproj
./build/release/bin/mercury_editor
@endcode

The launcher lists recent projects and has a **New Project** card. Enter a
name, choose a folder and pick **2D** or **3D**; the editor creates the
project, an empty map and the folders a project needs.

The main editor window has these panels:

| Panel | What it is for |
|---|---|
| **Hierarchy** | The objects in the current map. **Create** adds objects and child objects. |
| **Inspector** | The fields of the selected object and the components on it. **Add Component** adds another component or one of your scripts. |
| **Scene Viewport** | Where you select and move objects. Press W, E or R for move, rotate and scale handles; F frames the selection. |
| **Project Browser** | The project's files. **Create** makes maps, scripts, assets, UI documents and more. Double-click a file to open it. |
| **Console** | Engine and script output, including errors. Click a script location in an error to open that line. |
| **Script Editor** | The built-in AngelScript editor with completion and diagnostics. |
| **UI Builder**, **Input Actions**, **Lore VCS** | Optional windows under the **Window** menu. |

Useful menu commands:

- **File > Save** (Ctrl+S) saves the current map; **File > Set Current Map as
  Main Map** makes it the map that runs when the project starts.
- **GameObject** and the Hierarchy's **Create** menu add empty objects, 3D
  primitives, lights, cameras and 2D sprites.
- **Project > Build Scripts** (F7) compiles the AngelScript without running.
- **Project > Play / Stop** (F5) saves the map, builds the scripts and starts
  the game in its own process. The Console shows the game's output.
- **File > Build Game...** (Ctrl+B) produces a shippable folder.
- **File > Preferences...** switches between the built-in and an external
  script editor.

The editor never executes game scripts itself. It builds them and asks the
player for their fields (`--dump-schema`), which is what the Inspector uses to
show your `[field]` values.

@section getting_started_project A project on disk

A project is a folder with a `.mproj` file at its root. Every stored path is
relative to that folder and written as `res://...`:

@code{.ini}
my_game/
  my_game.mproj          project settings: name, main map, window size, ...
  maps/main.mmap         the main map: nodes and their components
  scripts/spinner.as     AngelScript gameplay code (optional)
  scripts/spinner.cc     C++ gameplay code (optional, needs a build target)
  assets/white.masset    materials, physics materials and other assets
  ui/hud.uxml            optional UI Toolkit documents
  .mercury/              generated (scripts schema and build caches)
@endcode

`.mercury/` and `Builds/` are generated. Do not commit them; new projects
include a `.gitignore` that ignores `.mercury/`.

A minimal `.mproj` is:

@code{.ini}
[project]
name = "HelloMercury"
main_map = "res://maps/main.mmap"

[window]
title = "Hello Mercury"
width = 1280
height = 720
@endcode

@section getting_started_first_script Your first AngelScript script

Scripts are AngelScript files in `scripts/`. A class marked `[script]` becomes
a component that you can add to a node. The class name is the name the map
uses; the file name has no special meaning.

Create `scripts/spinner.as`:

@code{.cpp}
// Rotate this object around its Y axis.

[script]
class Spinner : Behaviour
{
    [field tooltip="Degrees per second"]
    float speed = 90.0f;

    void onStart()
    {
        Debug::Log("Spinner started on " + GetGameObject().name);
    }

    void onFrame(double dt)
    {
        GameObject self = GetGameObject();
        Vector3 angles = self.eulerAngles;
        self.eulerAngles = Vector3(angles.x, angles.y + speed * dt, angles.z);
    }
}
@endcode

What each part does:

- `[script]` registers `Spinner` as a component.
- `float speed` wrapped in `[field]` is shown in the Inspector, saved in the
  map, and can be changed while designing the game.
- `onStart()` runs once, after every object in the map exists.
- `onFrame(double dt)` runs once per rendered frame. `dt` is the frame time in
  seconds, so `speed * dt` is independent of the frame rate.
- `onTick(double dt)` is the alternative for physics and simulation work. It
  runs at the project's fixed tick rate, before physics.
- `GetGameObject()` returns a handle to the object that owns the script.
  `name`, `position`, `eulerAngles` and `localScale` are properties on that
  handle.

Do not call engine functions from a script constructor; the object does not
exist yet. Use `onStart()` or `awake()` instead.

@section getting_started_add_script Add the script in the editor

1. Create `scripts/spinner.as` in the Project Browser (**Create > Script...**
   also writes a template) and press **F7** (**Project > Build Scripts**) so
   the new class appears in the Inspector's component list.
2. Open the project and select the object that should spin in the
   **Hierarchy**; the new 3D template already has a **Cube**.
3. In the **Inspector**, press **Add Component** and choose **Spinner**.
4. Set **Speed** to the value you want.
5. Press **F5** (**Project > Play / Stop**) to run it.

The Inspector writes a component section into the map, so the same object can
also be described by hand:

@code{.ini}
[node name="Cube" parent="."]

[component type="Transform" node="Cube"]
position = Vector3(0, 0.5, 0)

[component type="MeshRenderer" node="Cube"]

[component type="Spinner" node="Cube"]
speed = 45.0
@endcode

The `node` value is the path from the map root (`.` is the root itself). The
property names after `[component ...]` are exactly the field names in the
script. A key that no field has is reported as a warning when the project is
built, so a renamed field is caught early.

@section getting_started_run Run without the editor

The player executes any project, so you can share a project or run it from a
terminal:

@code{.sh}
./build/release/bin/mercury_player my_game/my_game.mproj
# start at another map
./build/release/bin/mercury_player my_game/my_game.mproj --map res://maps/level2.mmap
@endcode

If the scripts changed, the player compiles them before starting. Useful
options:

- `--build-scripts` compiles and exits (the same check as F7).
- `--dump-schema file` writes the fields of every script and exits; the editor
  uses this to build its Inspector.
- `--help` prints the usage.

When a script throws, the game keeps running: the error and its
`scripts/file.as:line:column` location appear in the Console (or terminal). A
call that runs longer than `MERCURY_SCRIPT_TIMEOUT` seconds (10 by default) is
aborted as an infinite loop.

@section getting_started_cpp Writing gameplay in C++

AngelScript is optional. A project can link C++ gameplay into its own
executable, and a project may mix C++ and AngelScript components. A C++ script
is a class deriving from @ref Mercury::Behaviour, registered with
`MERCURY_SCRIPT`:

@code{.cpp}
// scripts/spinner.h
#include "scripting/behaviour.h"

class Spinner : public Mercury::Behaviour {
 public:
  void Reflect(Mercury::Reflector& r) override {
    r.Field("speed", speed).Tooltip("Degrees per second");
  }
  void OnFrame(double dt) override {
    auto& transform = GetTransform();
    transform.SetRotate(transform.GetRotate() +
                        glm::vec3(0.0f, speed * static_cast<float>(dt), 0.0f));
  }
  float speed = 45.0f;
};
@endcode

@code{.cpp}
// scripts/spinner.cc
#include "spinner.h"

MERCURY_SCRIPT(Spinner);
@endcode

`Reflect` works exactly like `[field]` in AngelScript: it describes the
Inspector fields and the values saved in the map. The callbacks are the C++
names (`Awake`, `OnStart`, `OnFrame`, `OnLateFrame`, `OnTick`,
`OnCollisionEnter`, ...). A reflected struct registered with
`MERCURY_DATA_ASSET(Type)` is the C++ equivalent of `[dataAsset]`.

A C++ project builds its own player with the `mercury_add_game` CMake function:

@code{.cmake}
# my_game/CMakeLists.txt
mercury_add_game(my_game PROJECT "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}")
@endcode

The function compiles every `.cc`/`.cpp` under the package's `scripts/`
folder, links the engine, and generates a `main()` that calls
@ref Mercury::RunPlayer. Name the executable in the project file so the editor
knows how to build and run it:

@code{.ini}
[build]
target = "my_game"
@endcode

The editor's **Build Scripts** and **Play** then run `cmake --build` for the
target, ask the executable for its script list (`--dump-schema`), and launch
it. Without `[build] target`, the shared `mercury_player` compiles `.as`
scripts at run time as before. To run the C++ example:

@code{.sh}
./build/release/bin/cpp_hello
@endcode

@section getting_started_next Where to go next

- @ref editor_guide "Mercury Editor guide" -- every panel, Play/Build and
  shipping.
- @ref scripting_guide "Scripting guide" -- the script lifecycle, input,
  physics, UI, events and the full native API.
- @ref cpp_api "Using the C++ library" -- the engine's C++ entry points.
- The project
  <a href="md__d_o_c_u_m_e_n_t_a_t_i_o_n.html">DOCUMENTATION.md</a> describes the
  file formats, renderer, components and testing in depth.
- `examples/` contains complete, working projects to copy from.

*/
